 | EV Compensation | -3 to 3 | 0 | EV bias, +1 = one stop brighter. In Manual mode this is a true stop. |
 | Auto Exposure Lock | bool | false | **Scene Referred only.** Freeze the metered EV where it is, like a camera's AE lock. The aperture, ND and exposure compensation still apply on top. |
 | Auto Exposure Response Time | 0-10 s (slider 0-5) | 1.0 | **Scene Referred only.** Seconds for the metered EV to cover 63 % of a change. A camera cut always snaps instantly. 0 = no smoothing. |
+| Auto Darken Time | 0-10 s (slider 0-5) | 0.5 | **Scene Referred only.** Seconds to cover 63 % of a change when the scene gets **brighter** and the picture therefore has to darken - walking out of a hangar into the sun. Half of Response Time by default, because the problem is asymmetric and one time constant cannot serve both halves of it: a real camera stops down at once and the eye adapts to light in about a fifth of a second, while adapting to dark takes minutes. Response Time still governs the other direction. 0 = one constant for both, the old symmetric behaviour. |
+| Metering Mode | enum | Centre-weighted | **Scene Referred only.** Where in the frame the camera meters. **Average** reads the whole frame uniformly - the A/B reference, and outdoors the pattern that most often gets it wrong. **Centre-weighted** keeps full sensitivity in the middle and a quarter of it in the corners, which is what stops a bright sky from stopping the subject down. **Spot** reads a soft-edged disc a fifth of the frame height across (about 1.8 % of frame area), so you can put it on a face or a grey card and let everything else fall where it falls. |
+| Metering Band Low | 0-100 % | 50 | **Scene Referred only.** Discard everything below this percentile of metering weight before averaging. The camera meters a 64-bin histogram, not an average, and this is the bottom of the band it keeps. 50 throws away the darker half of the frame - shadow detail nobody meters for, and exactly what dragged an outdoor scene below mid grey. 0 keeps everything. |
+| Metering Band High | 0-100 % | 90 | **Scene Referred only.** Discard everything above this percentile. 90 throws away the top decile - sky, glints, the sun itself - so panning past the sun is a non-event instead of a stop-down. 100 keeps everything. The camera exposes so that the log-mean of what is **left** between the two lands on mid grey. Deliberately not Unreal's 10/90, which exists to make its histogram agree with its mean; the mean is the thing this replaces. |
 | Auto Exposure EV Min | -6 to 24 | -2.0 | **Scene Referred only.** The brightest the camera will open up to (low EV = open). |
 | Auto Exposure EV Max | -6 to 24 | 20.0 | **Scene Referred only.** The darkest the camera will stop down to (high EV = closed). |
 | Sensor Clip (stops over grey) | 8-20 | 14.0 | **Scene Referred only.** The sensor's full well. Highlights are clipped per channel at this many stops above mid grey, **after** the white balance, so a clipped highlight stays white at every colour temperature instead of taking the balance's tint - which is what every RAW converter does and why a blown sky in a real camera is white and not blue. 14 matches the exposure meter's own ceiling. Lower = a smaller, earlier-clipping sensor (more flat white sky); higher = highlight colour and structure a long way up. The lens effects upstream - bloom, glare, diffraction, depth of field - are deliberately **not** clipped: the lens sees the real light, so a sun a hundred thousand times over the clip still throws a flare that says so. |
