+### r_SceneReferredMeterHistogram
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 1 |
+| Purpose | **How** the camera's AUTO mode meters, while the scene-referred path is active (stage 2 and up). |
+
+`1` - the **64-bin log2 luminance histogram**. A compute pass over the quarter-res exposed frame
+bins every texel by how many stops it sits above or below mid grey (-14 to +18, half a stop per
+bin), weighted by the camera's **Metering Mode**, and the camera takes the log-mean of the
+percentile band between **Metering Band Low** and **Metering Band High**.
+
+`0` - the shipped **whole-frame log-mean** of the albedo-divided illuminance estimate. Kept as the
+A/B reference and as the fallback if the compute pass ever misbehaves. On `0` the camera's
+Metering Mode and metering band do nothing, and the meter's own 14-stop ceiling is back in force.
+
+The difference is a *statistic*, not a constant. A geometric mean is shifted by
+`f * log2(V_hot / V_bg)` for a fraction `f` of the frame - **linear in area fraction** - so it is
+nearly blind to the sun (0.025 % of a 50 mm frame, 0.0035 stops) and violently sensitive to the
+sky (half the frame). Outdoors that put 74 700 lux on mid grey when the subject was lit by
+100 000, and the whole picture landed in a 2.5-stop band below grey, in the toe of the output
+transform - which is why it read as *dark and flat with no sun/shadow separation*. A percentile is
+robust to that by construction; no amount of clamping can make a mean robust to it.
+
+Two consequences worth stating:
+
+- **What is metered changed.** The histogram meters **luminance**. The old meter divided the
+  G-buffer albedo out to estimate illuminance and *fabricated* a 0.2 albedo for every pixel with
+  no albedo - sky, emissive, everything forward-rendered - which over-reads by 1.6 to 3.2 stops
+  outside midday sun. That fabrication no longer exists and no longer matters. The meter is now a
+  **reflected** meter, like the one in a real body; what keeps the calibration is the *target*,
+  not the quantity, because a correctly exposed 18 % card still lands on 0.18.
+- **The meter can no longer run away.** `log2(+Inf)` lands in the top bin, so an overflowed texel
+  gets a *position* and no *magnitude*, and the band discards it. Where the old clamp had to give
+  an outlier a value - and therefore a vote - the histogram gives it only a place in the queue.
+
